-- allium: 3
--
-- Biscuit Games — play surfaces
--
-- Scope
--   The surfaces a Biscuit Games game is played on, as far as they are the
--   platform's: the cell that carries one unit of play and says what is known
--   about it, the keyboard of cells a reader presses to enter one, and the
--   primer that explains what the marks mean. The vocabulary of marks each cell
--   wears, the words a game supplies for them, and how far apart two of those
--   marks have to sit.
--
-- Includes
--   - The four marks a play cell can wear, named by the platform, and the
--     non-colour indication each one carries.
--   - That a game supplies the words for each mark and the platform never does,
--     because two games will not mean the same thing by one.
--   - The separations between those marks, as ratios rather than as colours:
--     between an unmarked cell and a marked one, and between the mark that
--     carries no hue and the strongest one that does.
--   - The on-screen keyboard as a surface of cells that are controls: a layout
--     supplied by whoever mounts it, keys that are real controls with names, and
--     completeness on its own.
--   - The primer that explains the marks, shown in the marks' own ink and
--     reachable again rather than shown once and lost.
--
-- Excludes
--   - Every arrangement. A board, a grid, a rack, a row and a ladder are
--     arrangements a game chooses; this module states what one cell owes and
--     never how many there are or where they sit.
--   - What any mark means. The platform names four; a game says what each one
--     is claiming, and supplies the sentence a reader hears.
--   - Colour values, palettes, markup and animation mechanics. Naming a
--     separation is not choosing an ink: this module says how far apart two
--     marks must be, never what either one is. src/app.css is what answers it.
--   - The legibility floors every surface owes the page, the exemption an
--     unavailable control carries, and how motion defers to the reader.
--     appearance.allium states all three, and a mark clears them as well as the
--     separations here.
--   - How a surface is operated. operation.allium states that, including the
--     size of a key and its place in its row, what a dialog owes, and which
--     bare keys a surface may claim from a physical keyboard.
--
-- Dependencies
--   None. This is one of the platform's root modules, and it imports nothing so
--   that a game can inherit these obligations whole rather than restate them.
--   appearance.allium and operation.allium are the others; none of the three
--   imports another, because they are peers describing different aspects of the
--   same surfaces rather than layers.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    -- One cell of the surface in front of the reader. A play surface is made of
    -- these, and this module never says how many or how they are arranged. It is
    -- declared rather than created because nothing here brings one into being,
    -- and every obligation below is owed of each one that exists.
    cell: PlayCell
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a surface has made of a cell's contents. Named for how strongly each
-- reads rather than for what any game means by it: the meaning is the game's and
-- arrives as words.
enum PlayMark {
    -- Nothing is known about this cell yet, or nothing has been put in it. Every
    -- play surface has this mark and every one starts in it, which is why it is
    -- named here rather than left as the absence of a mark: the separation below
    -- is a distance from it, and a figure cannot be stated about something the
    -- module will not name.
    unmarked
    -- The strongest claim a game makes about a cell. Carries a hue and the
    -- fullest non-colour indication of the three.
    | exact
    -- A claim that holds in part: what the cell carries counts for something,
    -- and not for this. Carries a second hue and a lesser indication.
    | present
    -- What the cell carries is out of play. The one mark with no hue and no
    -- indication of its own, which is why its absence is itself the indication
    -- and why the second separation below names it.
    | absent
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity PlayCell {
    -- What the surface is saying about whatever this cell carries.
    mark: PlayMark

    -- The words the game supplies for that mark, heard by anyone reading by ear
    -- rather than by eye. The platform names the marks and never the meanings:
    -- two games will not agree on what one of them is claiming, and both owe the
    -- reader a sentence.
    description: String

    is_marked: mark != unmarked

    -- A mark with no words is a colour on its own, which the contract below
    -- forbids outright. Stated as a field so it is a property of the cell rather
    -- than a hope about whoever drew it.
    --
    -- This is the mechanical form of the test and not the whole of it: the
    -- expression can compare against the empty string and has no way to say
    -- "blank", so a sentence of nothing but spaces satisfies it and is not words.
    -- EveryMarkIsNamedInWords carries that half, and governs where the two
    -- differ.
    is_told_in_words: description != ""
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- How far a cell nothing is known about has to sit from one that has been
    -- marked. No standard supplies this figure, because standards ask a colour
    -- to stand off its background rather than off another state, and a play
    -- surface's marks sit side by side.
    minimum_state_separation: Decimal = 3.0

    -- How far absent has to sit from exact. That pair and no other: present is
    -- left to its hue and its own indication, because it is the mark a reader
    -- sees least and the range is already spent. Lower than the figure above,
    -- and deliberately so: separating four marks by 3 to one each would need a
    -- range of 27 to one, which no palette has, and forcing it drives absent —
    -- the mark most of a played surface is in — to whichever extreme is left.
    -- This bar is what the range can afford once the one above is paid for, and
    -- it does not stand alone: these two are the pair a reader without hue has
    -- least to go on with, so the non-colour indication the contract below
    -- requires is doing the rest of the work here rather than merely confirming
    -- it. Whoever tunes a palette should satisfy the high-contrast palettes
    -- first, because pushing a mark further from the page for contrast's sake
    -- spends the same lightness range this separation is paid from.
    minimum_mark_separation: Decimal = 2.0
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every surface made of marked cells fulfils this, whether the cells are read or
-- pressed, which is why it is stated once here rather than on each of them.
contract Marking {
    @invariant AMarkIsNeverOnlyAColour
        -- Every mark carries a non-colour indication that distinguishes it from
        -- every other mark on the surface, and not merely from the page: exact
        -- the fullest, present a lesser one that differs from it at a glance,
        -- absent none at all, with the absence read together with its dimmed
        -- contents. This asks more than appearance.allium's clause about colour
        -- alone, which asks a state to be distinguishable at all; marks that sit
        -- side by side have to be distinguishable from each other, which is a
        -- bar no standard sets and no automated check can see.

    @invariant EveryMarkIsNamedInWords
        -- Every marked cell carries the game's own sentence for its mark, so
        -- what the shape says to the eye is said to a reader in words. A mark
        -- the game supplied no words for is not a mark the platform will draw.
        --
        -- A sentence of nothing but spaces is no words. It reaches a reader as
        -- silence exactly as the empty one does, and the two arrive by the same
        -- accidents — an interpolation that found nothing, a translation key
        -- with no entry, a join over an empty list — so the platform declines
        -- them alike. is_told_in_words above cannot say this, which is why it is
        -- said here: a cell is told in words when its sentence has a word in
        -- it, and drawing the mark is what the platform does only then.

    @invariant AnUnmarkedCellStandsOffAMarkedOne
        -- A cell nothing is known about hugs the page: the page's own ground, a
        -- drawn boundary, the page's ink. A marked cell answers in the way its
        -- mark affords, at least config.minimum_state_separation off the ground
        -- the unmarked cell hugs; and absent, which has no hue and no indication
        -- to speak for it, holds its contents at least that far from the
        -- unmarked contents. Every figure holds by lightness and not by hue
        -- alone, because the distinction a reader consults to know what is still
        -- open must survive a screen dimmed far enough to take the hues with it.

    @invariant AbsentStandsOffExact
        -- Absent stands off exact by at least config.minimum_mark_separation,
        -- carried by the boundary each of the two draws. The pair is the
        -- platform's to name because it is fixed by the marks rather than by any
        -- game's meanings: absent is the one mark with no hue and exact the
        -- strongest that has one. A distance, not a direction — which of the two
        -- is the lighter is the theme's to decide.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Cells that are read.
surface PlaySurface {
    exposes:
        cell.mark
        cell.description
        cell.is_marked
        cell.is_told_in_words

    contracts:
        fulfils Marking

    @guarantee ACellReadsAsOneThing
        -- A cell reaches assistive technology as a single named thing carrying
        -- its contents and its mark together, rather than as a character with a
        -- decoration announced beside it. Its indication is not separately
        -- announced, and a cell with nothing in it is still named.

    @guarantee TheArrangementIsTheGamesAndTheCellIsThePlatforms
        -- This module owns one cell and nothing about where cells sit. A grid, a
        -- row, a rack and a ladder are arrangements a game chooses, and each of
        -- them owes what is stated here of every cell it contains.
}

-- Cells that are pressed.
surface EntryKeyboard {
    exposes:
        cell.mark
        cell.description

    contracts:
        fulfils Marking

    @guarantee EveryKeyIsAControl
        -- Each key is a real control with an accessible name. A key showing a
        -- glyph and no words says its action in words instead, and a key
        -- carrying a mark says the mark with it.
        --
        -- The name comes from whichever of the layout's own fields has a word
        -- in it, and a field holding nothing but spaces has none. A blank is
        -- the shape a rack's blank tile takes, and a phrase game's space bar
        -- after it, so reading one as a name is how a key arrives with no name
        -- at all — the same test Marking applies to a mark's sentence, applied
        -- to a key's fields.
        --
        -- One case this guarantee does not reach, stated rather than left for
        -- an empty name to be discovered in. Where the layout supplies no words
        -- anywhere, the platform draws the key and leaves it unnamed: it will
        -- not invent a word for a key, and dropping the key instead would
        -- change what the row is, which is a worse answer than a control whose
        -- own author can see it is missing its word.

    @guarantee TheKeyboardIsCompleteOnItsOwn
        -- Every operation the surface offers through a bare key it has claimed
        -- from the physical keyboard is offered here as well, so the claim can
        -- always be dropped without taking anything away.

    @guarantee ALayoutIsSuppliedRatherThanFixed
        -- The arrangement of keys — how many rows, which cells sit in each, and
        -- which of them end a turn rather than build one — is supplied by
        -- whoever mounts the keyboard, and this module names no key. What holds
        -- whatever the layout is: every key is reachable by keyboard and by
        -- finger, the keys that end a turn are identifiable as such so the row
        -- rule knows which of them may be wider, and a layout with no keys is
        -- not a keyboard.
}

-- The explanation of what the marks mean.
surface Primer {
    exposes:
        cell.mark
        cell.description

    contracts:
        fulfils Marking

    @guarantee TheMarksAreShownInTheirOwnInk
        -- The example beside each sentence is the surface's own cell, wearing
        -- the mark it is explaining, in every theme and both palettes — not a
        -- picture of one. An explanation drawn separately is one that can come
        -- to disagree with the surface it describes. The examples are hidden
        -- from assistive technology, because the sentences beside them are the
        -- content and the cell's own name read out first would be noise.

    @guarantee TheExplanationIsReachableAgain
        -- However a reader first meets the explanation, it is reachable again
        -- afterwards rather than shown once and lost. Where it is reached as a
        -- dialog it owes everything operation.allium's Dialog states.

    @guarantee TheWordsAreTheGamesAndTheFrameIsThePlatforms
        -- The platform supplies the shape — a sentence per mark, each beside its
        -- own cell — and never a sentence. The words are the game's and are the
        -- same words wherever the explanation appears, so a reader who saw it on
        -- arrival and opens it again reads what they read before.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Three marks and unmarked is what the palette can afford: the separations above
-- spend most of the lightness range, and the reasoning for the second figure
-- says plainly that four marks three to one apart would need a range of 27 to
-- one. So a game whose play needs a fourth mark cannot simply be given one. What
-- it may do instead — collapse two of its states onto one mark, carry the fourth
-- on shape alone with no separation promised, or force a wider range at some
-- other cost — has not been decided, and the first game to need one should not
-- decide it by shipping.
open question "A fourth mark - what does the platform owe a game whose play needs more marks than the three stated here?"

-- Until now a game chose its own separations, so this could not arise. The
-- platform states them now, and a game that finds one of these figures wrong for
-- what it renders has nowhere to say so: there is no sanctioned form for "the
-- platform's figure, except here", and no gate would notice one being taken. The
-- same question is open about a token in docs/project/what-the-hub-owns.md, and
-- the two should be answered together or not at all.
open question "Forking a figure - may a game state a separation of its own in place of one stated here, and how would it say so?"
